          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"Modern-English rendering of Cordelia’s “I love your majesty”: “I love you.”\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state, while carefully limiting its extent to what she considers proper filial love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression weakly invokes Cordelia’s existing obligation as Lear’s daughter through “my bond”; it does not clearly create a new promise. Cordelia is the obligated party, and the invoked duties are filial love, obedience, and honour toward Lear.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The love expression is a controlled, qualified avowal rather than a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalizing her heart, but that does not make the resulting assertion an expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a measured refusal to make the unlimited, competitive declaration Lear demands. Its restrictive qualification implicitly rejects her sisters’ mode of answering.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal to her father, defining her love by her filial bond rather than claiming limitless love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes, rather than newly creates, her existing filial obligations to Lear: to love, obey, and honour him as is fitting. Lear does not accept the initial formulation as adequate; he tells her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes. The supplied passage does not establish any further uptake beyond that immediate challenge.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt supports analysis of the immediate exchange and supplies Cordelia’s asides as evidence of affection. It does not by itself settle every question about sincerity, later conduct, or the ultimate consequences and reception of the declaration.\"}"
